window.SEARCH_INDEX = [
{"title":"What an SDS is","page":"Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets","summary":"Every chemical and liquid in the shop has a Safety Data Sheet. Read it before you use the material, not after.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/chemicals-and-sds.html#what-an-sds-is","text":"What an SDS is A Safety Data Sheet is required documentation for every chemical and liquid used in the shop. It carries the critical safety information: hazards, handling procedures, first aid and emergency response . It is a legal requirement under OSHA regulations - not a local rule."},
{"title":"Finding one","page":"Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets","summary":"Every chemical and liquid in the shop has a Safety Data Sheet. Read it before you use the material, not after.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/chemicals-and-sds.html#finding-one","text":"Finding one All SDS sheets are organized in a designated binder for easy reference. Review before use - check the SDS for any unfamiliar chemical or liquid. Ask the instructor if you cannot locate an SDS for a material. Your responsibility: know the hazards of any chemical you are using, and follow the proper handling procedures."},
{"title":"Storage","page":"Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets","summary":"Every chemical and liquid in the shop has a Safety Data Sheet. Read it before you use the material, not after.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/chemicals-and-sds.html#storage","text":"Storage Flammable liquids must be stored in approved fireproof cabinets when not in use. Never store incompatible chemicals together. All containers must be properly labeled. Fumes and dust have a PPE requirement of their own - see [[ppe|respiratory protection]]."},
{"title":"Prohibited behaviour","page":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","summary":"What is never acceptable in a lab, what alertness actually requires, and what to do when something goes wrong.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/conduct-and-emergency.html#prohibited-behaviour","text":"Prohibited behaviour NEVER operate equipment under the influence of drugs, alcohol, or medication that causes drowsiness. NO horseplay, pranks or roughhousing in any lab area. Do not distract others who are operating equipment. No unauthorized experiments or modifications to equipment."},
{"title":"Staying alert","page":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","summary":"What is never acceptable in a lab, what alertness actually requires, and what to do when something goes wrong.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/conduct-and-emergency.html#staying-alert","text":"Staying alert Stay focused - maintain concentration while operating equipment. Take breaks when tired or distracted. Ask questions if you are unsure about any procedure. Report unsafe conditions immediately to the instructor."},
{"title":"Emergency procedures","page":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","summary":"What is never acceptable in a lab, what alertness actually requires, and what to do when something goes wrong.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/conduct-and-emergency.html#emergency-procedures","text":"Emergency procedures Know the location of emergency stops, fire extinguishers, first aid kits and exits . Call 911 for serious injuries or fires. Notify the instructor immediately of any incident, injury, or near-miss. Do not move a seriously injured person unless they are in immediate danger. For electrical shock specifically, the first action is different - see [[electrical-safety|If someone is shocked]]."},
{"title":"Workspace responsibility","page":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","summary":"What is never acceptable in a lab, what alertness actually requires, and what to do when something goes wrong.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/conduct-and-emergency.html#workspace-responsibility","text":"Workspace responsibility Clean as you go - do not wait until the end. Report equipment problems before they become safety hazards."},
{"title":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","page":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","summary":"What is never acceptable in a lab, what alertness actually requires, and what to do when something goes wrong.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/conduct-and-emergency.html#section-5","text":"Injury or emergency - Call 911 - or Murray State Public Safety at 270-809-2222"},
{"title":"Voltage in this lab","page":"Electrical Safety","summary":"Voltages present in the lab, the inspection required before every use, and what to do when someone is shocked.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/electrical-safety.html#voltage-in-this-lab","text":"Voltage in this lab 110 V Household voltage. Still dangerous - can cause serious injury or death. 220 V / 480 V Industrial equipment. Extremely dangerous - often fatal. Never assume any voltage is \"safe\"."},
{"title":"Inspection - mandatory before each use","page":"Electrical Safety","summary":"Voltages present in the lab, the inspection required before every use, and what to do when someone is shocked.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/electrical-safety.html#inspection-mandatory-before-each-use","text":"Inspection - mandatory before each use Check for frayed, cracked or exposed wiring. Look for damaged plugs or connectors. Inspect extension cords for cuts or wear. If you find damage, discontinue use immediately and notify the instructor."},
{"title":"Safe practice","page":"Electrical Safety","summary":"Voltages present in the lab, the inspection required before every use, and what to do when someone is shocked.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/electrical-safety.html#safe-practice","text":"Safe practice Always disconnect power before performing any maintenance or adjustment. Never yank or pull a cord - grasp the plug, not the cord. Keep electrical connections away from water and wet surfaces. Be aware of overhead wiring and floor-level cords, and avoid contact. Lockout/Tagout for machinery with rotating parts is covered in [[machine-hazards]]."},
{"title":"If someone is shocked","page":"Electrical Safety","summary":"Voltages present in the lab, the inspection required before every use, and what to do when someone is shocked.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/electrical-safety.html#if-someone-is-shocked","text":"If someone is shocked Do NOT touch them directly. Turn off the power source first, or use a non-conductive object to separate them from it. Then follow [[conduct-and-emergency|emergency procedure]] - call 911 and notify the instructor."},
{"title":"Three categories, one question","page":"Equipment Categories and Supervision","summary":"Every machine in every lab falls into one of three categories. The category tells you who has to be standing there before you switch it on.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-categories.html#three-categories-one-question","text":"Three categories, one question Every piece of equipment in the DET labs is Category 1, 2 or 3. The category answers one question: who has to be present before you switch it on? Permission from an instructor is required in every case. What changes between categories is when that permission can be given, and who has to be in the room while the machine is running. Category 1 Permission may be given in advance. Work alone once you have it. Category 2 Permission at the time of use. A peer must be present throughout. Category 3 Permission at the time of use. A peer and an instructor must be present throughout. The category never removes a requirement. General safety procedures and PPE apply to every category, to every person present - see [[equipment-operating-procedure]] and [[ppe]]."},
{"title":"Category 1","page":"Equipment Categories and Supervision","summary":"Every machine in every lab falls into one of three categories. The category tells you who has to be standing there before you switch it on.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-categories.html#category-1","text":"Category 1 Can be used with instructor permission - may be given in advance or at the time of use. Can be operated independently , without peer or instructor supervision, once permission is granted. You must still follow all general safety procedures and PPE requirements."},
{"title":"Category 2","page":"Equipment Categories and Supervision","summary":"Every machine in every lab falls into one of three categories. The category tells you who has to be standing there before you switch it on.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-categories.html#category-2","text":"Category 2 Can be used with instructor permission - permission must be given at the time of use . Does not require instructor supervision during operation. Requires peer supervision present at all times during use. Both the operator and the peer supervisor must follow all safety procedures and PPE requirements."},
{"title":"Category 3","page":"Equipment Categories and Supervision","summary":"Every machine in every lab falls into one of three categories. The category tells you who has to be standing there before you switch it on.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-categories.html#category-3","text":"Category 3 Can be used with instructor permission - permission must be given at the time of use . Requires both peer AND instructor supervision present during operation. The highest-risk equipment, requiring maximum safety oversight. All participants - operator, peer and instructor - must follow safety procedures and PPE requirements."},
{"title":"Which category is my machine?","page":"Equipment Categories and Supervision","summary":"Every machine in every lab falls into one of three categories. The category tells you who has to be standing there before you switch it on.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-categories.html#which-category-is-my-machine","text":"Which category is my machine? The category assignment is per lab. Those rosters are being brought across from the existing slides and are not published here yet: [[woods-lab-equipment|Woods / Construction lab]] [[rpc-lab-equipment|Rapid Prototyping Center]] [[metals-lab-equipment|Metals lab]] Until they are, ask the instructor. A machine you cannot place is a machine you do not start."},
{"title":"The procedure that never changes","page":"Equipment Operating Procedure","summary":"The procedure that applies to every piece of equipment in every lab, every time you use it.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-operating-procedure.html#the-procedure-that-never-changes","text":"The procedure that never changes This applies to any equipment, in any lab, on every use - whatever its [[equipment-categories|category]]. Three phases. Nothing in them is optional, and nothing in them depends on what you are making."},
{"title":"Before operating any equipment","page":"Equipment Operating Procedure","summary":"The procedure that applies to every piece of equipment in every lab, every time you use it.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-operating-procedure.html#before-operating-any-equipment","text":"Before operating any equipment Obtain permission to operate the equipment from the instructor. Verify training - ensure ALL operators have received proper equipment training. Check PPE - confirm you are wearing all required personal protective equipment. See [[ppe]]. Perform a pre-use inspection - check for any required maintenance or safety issues. Confirm safety guards are in place and functioning properly. Secure the workpiece - ensure all material is properly clamped or held. Communicate your intentions - tell others in the area what you are about to do."},
{"title":"During equipment operation","page":"Equipment Operating Procedure","summary":"The procedure that applies to every piece of equipment in every lab, every time you use it.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-operating-procedure.html#during-equipment-operation","text":"During equipment operation Stay alert - maintain focus, and never leave equipment unattended while it is running. Follow proper procedures - use equipment only as trained and as intended. Machine-specific hazards - pinch points, measurement, housekeeping - are covered in [[machine-hazards]]."},
{"title":"After operating any equipment","page":"Equipment Operating Procedure","summary":"The procedure that applies to every piece of equipment in every lab, every time you use it.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-operating-procedure.html#after-operating-any-equipment","text":"After operating any equipment Power down safely - turn off and disconnect the equipment from its power source. Report issues immediately - notify the instructor of any broken or malfunctioning equipment. Return and organize - put all equipment and tools back in their designated locations. Restore to shutdown state - return the equipment to its original, safe configuration. Clean thoroughly - remove all dust, chips, liquids and debris from the equipment and the work area. The habit behind the last two is [[5s-workplace-organization|5S]] - the same discipline, named and made routine."},
{"title":"This page has not been written yet","page":"Fire Safety","summary":"Extinguisher classes, when to fight a fire and when to leave. Not yet written.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/fire-safety.html#this-page-has-not-been-written-yet","text":"This page has not been written yet Fire safety is a universal rule and belongs in this module. There is no source text for it. The \"Fire Safety\" slide in General Safety.pptx (slide 15) carries two images and no text at all - whatever was said about it was said out loud. So there is nothing to carry across, and this page has to be authored from scratch rather than converted. It is deliberately excluded from the General Safety playlist until it has content. A projected placeholder is worse than an acknowledged gap."},
{"title":"What it needs to cover","page":"Fire Safety","summary":"Extinguisher classes, when to fight a fire and when to leave. Not yet written.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/fire-safety.html#what-it-needs-to-cover","text":"What it needs to cover A starting outline, for the instructor to confirm or replace - none of this is established policy yet: Extinguisher classes present in each lab, and which fire each one is for. The decision rule: when to use an extinguisher, and when to evacuate instead. Extinguisher and alarm pull-station locations, per lab. Evacuation routes and the assembly point. Hot-work rules - welding, grinding, torch - and any permit or watch requirement. Flammable storage, which currently lives in [[chemicals-and-sds|chemical safety]]."},
{"title":"Safe manual handling","page":"Lifting and Carrying","summary":"Team lifting above 40 lbs, and the technique that prevents the injury. Most back injuries come from poor technique, not from heavy loads.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/lifting-and-carrying.html#safe-manual-handling","text":"Safe manual handling Most back injuries result from poor lifting technique, not from excessive weight. Team lifting is required for Anything over 40 lbs . Items lighter than 40 lbs but awkward in shape, size or balance. Any object that feels unstable or is difficult to grip."},
{"title":"Proper lifting technique","page":"Lifting and Carrying","summary":"Team lifting above 40 lbs, and the technique that prevents the injury. Most back injuries come from poor technique, not from heavy loads.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/lifting-and-carrying.html#proper-lifting-technique","text":"Proper lifting technique Plan the lift - clear your path and know where you are going. Get close to the object before lifting. Squat down using your leg muscles; keep your back straight. Grip firmly with both hands; avoid awkward hand positions. Lift smoothly using your legs, not your back. Keep the load close to your body throughout the lift. Setting it down Use the same technique in reverse - squat down, do not bend over. Place objects gently. Do not drop them."},
{"title":"Rotating machinery","page":"Machine Hazards and Housekeeping","summary":"Rotating machinery, the environment around it, and the operating habits that keep both from hurting you.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/machine-hazards.html#rotating-machinery","text":"Rotating machinery Gears, pulleys and belts, chains and sprockets are extremely dangerous pinch points. NEVER work on equipment with these features without first disconnecting power. Lockout/Tagout procedures must be followed for maintenance. Loose clothing, jewelry and unsecured hair are what these features catch - see [[ppe|clothing, jewelry and hair]]."},
{"title":"The environment around the machine","page":"Machine Hazards and Housekeeping","summary":"Rotating machinery, the environment around it, and the operating habits that keep both from hurting you.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/machine-hazards.html#the-environment-around-the-machine","text":"The environment around the machine Slip hazards Clean dust, metal chips, water and coolant off the floor immediately. Lighting Ensure lighting is adequate before operating any machine. Walkways Keep aisles and emergency exits unobstructed."},
{"title":"Operating habits","page":"Machine Hazards and Housekeeping","summary":"Rotating machinery, the environment around it, and the operating habits that keep both from hurting you.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/machine-hazards.html#operating-habits","text":"Operating habits Stop machines completely before taking measurements or making adjustments. Secure all workpieces before operation - use clamps, vises or fixtures. Never leave tools on or near operating machinery. Use proper tools - gloves or rags when handling sharp objects such as cutters. The shutdown and cleaning steps that close every session are in [[equipment-operating-procedure|After operating any equipment]]."},
{"title":"Personal Protective Equipment","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the woods and metals lab at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#personal-protective-equipment","text":"Personal Protective Equipment Five topics. One of them - eye protection - applies the moment you walk in. The rest are set by what you are doing. Eyes Always, in the woods and metals lab. No exceptions. Hearing Prolonged exposure to loud equipment. Feet Closed-toe shoes, always. Respiratory Any grinding or sanding; hazardous dust or fumes. Clothing & hair Always, near anything that rotates."},
{"title":"Eye protection","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the woods and metals lab at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#eye-protection","text":"Eye protection Safety glasses are MANDATORY in the woods and metals lab at all times. If you wear prescription glasses Side shields that attach to your existing frames. Oversized safety glasses that fit over corrective glasses. Prescription safety glasses are also acceptable. Lens requirements Safety glasses must be clear or yellow tinted only . Dark or shaded safety glasses are not acceptable for indoor lab use. Must meet ANSI Z87.1 safety standards."},
{"title":"Hearing protection","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the woods and metals lab at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#hearing-protection","text":"Hearing protection Industry standard: shop tools nearly always require hearing protection in professional settings. In this lab the exposure is typically shorter than in industry, but the protection still matters. When to use it Required for prolonged exposure to loud equipment - grinders, sanders, planers and similar. Two types available Earplugs Small foam plugs inserted into the ear canal. Disposable or reusable. Available on request from the instructor. Ear muffs Over-ear cups covering the whole ear. Reusable and adjustable. Often preferred for intermittent use. Both types significantly reduce harmful noise levels."},
{"title":"Foot protection","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the woods and metals lab at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#foot-protection","text":"Foot protection Minimum requirement: closed-toe shoes are MANDATORY - no sandals, no flip-flops, no open-toe footwear. Recommended upgrades Slip-resistant soles, for traction on surfaces that may be slippery. Puncture-resistant soles, against sharp metal debris. Steel-toe or composite-toe protection - recommended, not required. Avoid Canvas shoes or thin-soled footwear. High heels or unstable footwear. Anything that does not fully enclose your feet. You will be standing for most of the lab period - choose something supportive and comfortable."},
{"title":"Respiratory protection","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the woods and metals lab at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#respiratory-protection","text":"Respiratory protection MANDATORY when using any grinder or sander, and when working with materials that produce hazardous dust or fumes. Grinding galvanized material is PROHIBITED. Zinc fumes from galvanized steel are toxic when heated. Health hazards Metal dust and particles cause respiratory irritation and long-term lung damage. Wood dust can cause allergic reactions."},
{"title":"Respiratory protection - what to wear","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the woods and metals lab at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#respiratory-protection-what-to-wear","text":"Respiratory protection - what to wear N95 mask Filters 95% of airborne particles. The minimum acceptable standard. Approved respirator Half-face or full-face, for extended exposure. If you can smell fumes or taste particles, your protection is inadequate. Stop work immediately and upgrade it. Chemicals and liquids have their own documentation - see [[chemicals-and-sds]]."},
{"title":"Clothing and jewelry","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the woods and metals lab at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#clothing-and-jewelry","text":"Clothing and jewelry Loose clothing and accessories catch in rotating machinery. That is how this causes serious injury or death - it is not a dress code. Prohibited Loose-fitting clothing, or garments with dangling features - drawstrings, ties and similar. Long sleeves, hoodies or jackets near rotating equipment. ALL jewelry , except silicone wedding rings. Neckties, scarves and lanyards."},
{"title":"Hair, and what to wear instead","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the woods and metals lab at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#hair-and-what-to-wear-instead","text":"Hair, and what to wear instead Hair Long hair must be secured back and kept away from equipment. Use hair ties, clips or caps to prevent entanglement. Recommended Cotton or fire-retardant materials - preferred, not required. Close-fitting, short-sleeved shirts. Pants without excessive loose material."},
{"title":"Why 5S sits in a safety module","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#why-5s-sits-in-a-safety-module","text":"Why 5S sits in a safety module 5S is a lean manufacturing method for organizing a workplace. It earns its place here because most of what it asks for is also what keeps people from getting hurt. A cluttered bench hides a hazard. An unlabeled container is an unknown chemical. A floor with chips on it is a slip. Every one of those is a 5S failure before it is an incident - which is why the shutdown steps in [[equipment-operating-procedure|After operating any equipment]] are 5S in everything but name. This page is the basics. 5S will get its own expanded treatment later."},
{"title":"Sort - Seiri","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#sort-seiri","text":"Sort - Seiri Separate necessary items from unnecessary ones. Remove or relocate items that do not belong in the work area. Keep only what you need for the task in front of you."},
{"title":"Set in Order - Seiton","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#set-in-order-seiton","text":"Set in Order - Seiton Organize and label the remaining items clearly, for easy identification. A place for everything, and everything in its place. Create logical, efficient storage systems."},
{"title":"Shine - Seiso","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#shine-seiso","text":"Shine - Seiso Clean work areas to maintain high hygiene standards. Reduce accident risk through cleanliness and organization. Inspect while cleaning - that is how problems get found early. This is the step that overlaps most directly with safety - see [[machine-hazards|the environment around the machine]]."},
{"title":"Standardize - Seiketsu","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#standardize-seiketsu","text":"Standardize - Seiketsu Establish consistent procedures for maintaining the first three S's. Create habits through regular practice and routine. Ensure everyone follows the same organizational systems."},
{"title":"Sustain - Shitsuke","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#sustain-shitsuke","text":"Sustain - Shitsuke Continuously review and maintain all 5S practices. Build the discipline to follow established procedures long-term. Regular audits, and improvements to the system itself."}
];
